Yes — and the prime/composite analogy makes the insight cleaner.

An afterglow chimera is a composite. What feels like one unified ideal may actually factor into smaller “prime” attractions or admired qualities:

\text{Ideal} \rightarrow
\text{beauty}\times
\text{courage}\times
\text{wit}\times
\text{status}\times
\text{warmth}\times\cdots

You encounter those factors embodied in different people, times, and contexts. Memory then multiplies selected factors together and presents the result back to consciousness as though it were one person.

So instead of asking:

“Why can’t I find/be this person?”

Pantheon can factor the chimera:

“What are the irreducible things I am actually responding to?”

That is much more useful. Maybe the “perfect person” turns out to be six independent desires accidentally bound together. Maybe your heroic role model is really courage × competence × humor × loyalty, and no single source owns any of those properties.

There’s also a beautiful inversion:

factorization: chimera → primes
synthesis: primes → new composite

Pantheon can move both directions. Decompose an idol until you discover its operators, then recombine those operators intentionally into something attainable.

The mathematical caveat matters: integers have unique prime factorization; human meanings probably do not. The same behavior can often be factored several legitimate ways depending on semantic resolution. That actually makes the Pantheon version richer: the “primes” are resolution-dependent irreducibles—things you currently cannot usefully decompose further.

So this may connect the two ideas we’ve been circling:

Don’t confuse the composite with a fundamental. Factor it.

And perhaps even more sharply:

We fall in love with composites and learn from primes.

Then Pantheon gets to do the reverse: find the primes, choose the good ones, and build the chimera on purpose.